18,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Ethan Kingston, a 77-year-old woman, has uncomplicated Mondor disease and authorizes relevant information sharing for a treating specialist's consultation. The referral coordinator offers to send everything because selecting relevant records would involve reading them. The specialist requests the history and findings needed for this visit. The team has the authorization and is preparing the information to share."}],"tutorialEligible":false,"routineEligible":true,"earliestFacilityStage":0,"requiredClinicalSetting":"clinic","requiredCapabilityIds":[],"rewardTierId":"reward.clinic_basic","sourceLabels":["Confidentiality","Mondor Disease","Mondor's Disease: A Review of the Literature"],"decisionNodes":[{"id":"node.gs028se.p2e.confidentiality.v4.mondor.1","questionVariantId":"question.statistics-ethics.confidentiality.v4","primaryConceptId":"concept.statistics-ethics.confidentiality","stem":"How should the clinic handle the treating specialist's relevant information request after Ethan Kingston authorizes Mondor-disease care coordination?","answerChoices":[{"id":"key-4","label":"Share relevant information for authorized care","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"The patient has authorized coordination, and the specialist requests relevant information. Share for that purpose while avoiding unnecessary disclosure.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"wrong-4-2","label":"Request a new authorization for each relevant chart item","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The supplied authorization already supports relevant coordination. Treating every relevant item as newly prohibited obstructs that authorized care purpose.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"wrong-4-1","label":"Send the complete chart for the specialist to filter","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The whole chart may include information beyond the consultation's purpose. Authorization to coordinate does not justify unnecessary disclosure for the specialist to filter later.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"wrong-4-3","label":"Send a de-identified summary for the treating consultation","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The specialist needs relevant patient-specific consultation information. A de-identified substitute does not provide the authorized coordination requested in this scenario.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"Share relevant patient-specific information for the authorized consultation, avoiding unnecessary disclosure. The stated authorization supports that coordination; confidentiality does not require blocking every exchange or sending the entire chart.","teachingPoint":"Confidentiality permits appropriate exchange for authorized care; it does not prohibit all coordination. Share information relevant to the consultation while avoiding unnecessary disclosure. Remember to match the information to its purpose. Sending the whole chart for someone else to filter, or treating every relevant item as newly forbidden, misses that balance in this supplied setting.","sourceLabels":["Confidentiality","Mondor Disease","Mondor's Disease: A Review of the Literature"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[]},{"id":"node.gs028se.p2e.confidentiality.v4.mondor.2","questionVariantId":"question.mondor-disease.management.supportive-care.v1.p2e.confidentiality.v4","primaryConceptId":"concept.mondor-disease.supportive-management","currentUpdate":"The team shares relevant information for Ethan Kingston's authorized consultation while avoiding unnecessary disclosure. The specialist confirms concordant uncomplicated Mondor disease after appropriate clinical evaluation. There is no suspicious mass, infection, progressive diffuse breast change, adenopathy, recurrent or migratory thrombophlebitis, or known hypercoagulable condition. The same patient wants to discuss initial care now that relevant findings are available to the treating team.","stem":"Which initial care plan best fits Ethan Kingston's confirmed uncomplicated Mondor disease after appropriate evaluation, without infection, suspicious findings or a separate indication for anticoagulation?","answerChoices":[{"id":"supportive_care","label":"Warm compresses, appropriate NSAID, and follow-up","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"Supportive measures and follow-up fit the confirmed uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis after appropriate evaluation.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"therapeutic_anticoagulation","label":"Routine therapeutic anticoagulation","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Routine therapeutic anticoagulation is not standard for uncomplicated Mondor disease without a separate indication.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"venous_excision","label":"Surgical excision of the involved superficial vein","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Excision of the superficial vein is not routine initial treatment for concordant uncomplicated Mondor disease.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"incision_and_drainage","label":"Incision and drainage with empiric antibiotics","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The evaluated finding is uncomplicated Mondor disease, without an abscess or infection requiring drainage and antibiotics.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"Offer supportive care for concordant uncomplicated Mondor disease, including warm compresses, an NSAID when appropriate and follow-up. Other treatments require a separate indication.","teachingPoint":"Concordant uncomplicated Mondor disease is generally self-limited. Initial care is supportive with warm compresses, an NSAID when appropriate, avoidance of irritating activity or clothing, and follow-up. Antibiotics, venous excision, and therapeutic anticoagulation are not routine without a separate indication.","sourceLabels":["Mondor Disease","Mondor's Disease: A Review of the Literature","Confidentiality"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[{"answerChoiceId":"incision_and_drainage","kind":"no_terminal_outcome","consequenceNa