nd the potentially reversible medication-associated contribution before committing to irreversible therapy.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b03.d2","label":"Distal-spasm pattern; coordinate opioid withdrawal and reassessment before myotomy planning","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Notices the medication issue but ignores the abnormal junction relaxation when classifying the tracing.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"The failed/premature body activity with impaired junction relaxation is an achalasia-like spastic pattern. Opioids can produce this phenotype; Chicago v4 advises studying off opioids when possible. Coordinate a clinically appropriate withdrawal/reassessment plan before treating this as established primary disease requiring myotomy. No abrupt cessation or fixed washout is prescribed.","teachingPoint":"The failed/premature body activity with impaired junction relaxation is an achalasia-like spastic pattern. Opioids can produce this phenotype; Chicago v4 advises studying off opioids when possible. Coordinate a clinically appropriate withdrawal/reassessment plan before treating this as established primary disease requiring myotomy. No abrupt cessation or fixed washout is prescribed.","exhibit":{"kind":"keyValue","caption":"Constructed esophageal pressure-study observations; reference ranges are laboratory-specific.","items":[{"label":"Junction measurement","value":"Median relaxation pressure above the laboratory reference range"},{"label":"Most swallows","value":"An early distal contraction begins before the laboratory's latency reference; no normal propagating sequence occurs"},{"label":"Remaining swallows","value":"No distal contraction develops"}]},"sourceLabels":["Seoul achalasia consensus guideline (2020), CC BY-NC 4.0","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v4)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v5)","AI-assisted revision: needs