nuing as planned."},{"id":"profile.gs028se.impaired-colleague-research-consent.b-research-consent-1.male.46","prototypeDemographics":{"ageYears":46,"sexLabel":"Male"},"prototypeVitalSigns":{"heartRateBpm":76,"systolicBloodPressureMmHg":118,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Colin Lennox, a 46-year-old man, has a gastric GIST and is discussing specialist tissue and operative planning. A recruiter offers an optional study of a new biologic and says ordinary care requires enrollment. That study is not required for clinical care. The patient asks whether declining research would prevent the clinical consultation from continuing as planned."},{"id":"profile.gs028se.impaired-colleague-research-consent.b-research-consent-1.female.71","prototypeDemographics":{"ageYears":71,"sexLabel":"Female"},"prototypeVitalSigns":{"heartRateBpm":76,"systolicBloodPressureMmHg":118,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Colin Lennox, a 71-year-old woman, has a gastric GIST and is discussing specialist tissue and operative planning. A recruiter offers an optional study of a new biologic and says ordinary care requires enrollment. That study is not required for clinical care. The patient asks whether declining research would prevent the clinical consultation from continuing as planned."},{"id":"profile.gs028se.impaired-colleague-research-consent.b-research-consent-1.male.71","prototypeDemographics":{"ageYears":71,"sexLabel":"Male"},"prototypeVitalSigns":{"heartRateBpm":76,"systolicBloodPressureMmHg":118,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Colin Lennox, a 71-year-old man, has a gastric GIST and is discussing specialist tissue and operative planning. A recruiter offers an optional study of a new biologic and says ordinary care requires enrollment. That study is not required for clinical care. The patient asks whether declining research would prevent the clinical consultation from continuing as planned."}],"tutorialEligible":false,"routineEligible":true,"earliestFacilityStage":1,"requiredClinicalSetting":"clinic","requiredCapabilityIds":[],"rewardTierId":"reward.clinic_basic","sourceLabels":["Informed Consent in Research","Informed Consent","2023 GEIS Guidelines for gastrointestinal stromal tumors","Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors Treatment (PDQ®), Health Professional Version"],"decisionNodes":[{"id":"node.gs028se.p2e.research-consent.v1.gist.1","questionVariantId":"question.statistics-ethics.research-consent.v1","primaryConceptId":"concept.statistics-ethics.research-consent","stem":"What should the team explain when Colin Lennox is told that ordinary gastric-GIST care depends on joining an optional new-biologic study?","answerChoices":[{"id":"wrong-1-2","label":"Declining enrollment postpones the surgical consultation","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Postponing the ordinary consultation because enrollment is declined still makes care conditional on joining. That conflicts with voluntary choice in this setting.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"wrong-1-1","label":"Declining enrollment requires transfer to another clinic","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Requiring transfer would impose a loss of ordinary care for declining this optional study. The scenario gives no basis for making that care contingent on enrollment.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"wrong-1-3","label":"Declining enrollment limits access to routine follow-up","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Limiting routine follow-up uses ordinary care to pressure enrollment. The optional study is not required for that care.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"key-1","label":"Declining enrollment does not forfeit ordinary care","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"This study is optional and not required for ordinary care. Clarify that declining enrollment does not forfeit that care, so the invitation can be considered voluntarily.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"Clarify that declining this optional study does not forfeit ordinary clinic care. The recruiter's stated condition is inconsistent with voluntary participation in a study not required for that care.","teachingPoint":"Research participation must be voluntary. When a study is optional and not required for ordinary care, declining it should not forfeit that care. Remember to separate the clinical care decision from the research invitation. Making enrollment a condition of routine care pressures the patient instead of supporting an independent choice about participation.","sourceLabels":["Informed Consent in Research","Informed Consent","2023 GEIS Guidelines for gastrointestinal stromal tumors","Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors Treatment (PDQ®), Health Professional Version"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[]},{"id":"node.gs028se.p2e.research-consent.v1.gist.2","questionVariantId":"question.gastric-gist.mutation-guided-neoadjuvant-imatinib.v1.p2e.research-consent.v1","primaryConceptId":"concept.gastric-gist.mutation-guided-neoadjuvant-imatinib","currentUpdate":"The team explains that Colin Lennox's ordinary gastric-GIST care does not depend on joining the optional study. The specialist reviews completed core pathology and molecular testing showing GIST with an imatinib-sensitive mutation and no metastatic disease. The large posterior fundus mass abuts the pancreas; the multidisciplinary team expects meaningful shrinkage could avoid combined gastric and pancreatic resection.","stem":"Which multidisciplinary treatment plan best fits Colin Lennox's imatinib-sensitive, nonmetastatic gastric GIST when tumor shrinkage could reduce the morbidity of the planned extensive resection?","answerChoices":[{"id":"observe_1","label":"Offer symptom-directed care without tumor treatment","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Symptom-only care does not address this large, locally advanced lesion.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"imatinib_1"