02f.b03-context.d1","label":"Retain the reserve conclusion; revise the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"This accepts suppression as proof of reserve and lets symptom reproduction override the actual junction and pressure observations during loading.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102f.b03-context.d2","label":"Revise the reserve conclusion; revise the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The reserve conclusion needs correction, but the symptom-provoking liquid load does not itself supply evidence for the added outflow revision.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"manometry_3","label":"Revise the reserve conclusion; retain the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"Normal inhibition during the sequence is not evidence of post-sequence augmentation. The repeat responses challenge the reserve conclusion, whereas reproduced discomfort without the supplied outflow findings does not establish liquid-load obstruction.","serviceRequest":{"serviceId":"service.esophageal_manometry"}},{"id":"choice.qq20261102f.b03-context.d3","label":"Retain the reserve conclusion; retain the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Baseline propagation and appropriate inhibition do not establish the post-sequence reserve response. Accepting the whole draft misses that distinction.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"A provocative protocol has distinct acquisition phases. Appropriate inhibition while swallowing does not establish augmentation after the sequence; the repeated post-sequence finding warrants correction of the reserve statement. A familiar symptom during rapid drinking does not by itself establish impaired outflow. This report review does not assign a final motor diagnosis or a cause of all symptoms.","teachingPoint":"A provocative protocol has distinct acquisition phases. Appropriate inhibition while swallowing does not establish augmentation after the sequence; the repeated post-sequence finding warrants correction of the reserve statement. A familiar symptom during rapid drinking does not by itself establish impaired outflow. This report review does not assign a final motor diagnosis or a cause of all symptoms.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Constructed observations from an earlier study; laboratory comparisons are qualitative.","columnHeaders":["Separated swallows","Rapid-swallow sequences","Upright rapid drinking"],"rowHeaders":["Esophageal-body response","Junction and symptoms"],"cells":[["Propagated contractions between swallows","Pressure falls during the sequence; neither repeat is followed by a contraction exceeding the baseline response","No pressure column develops across the esophageal body"],["Relaxation within the laboratory range","Relaxation during the sequence within the laboratory range","Relaxation within the laboratory range; the familiar chest discomfort occurs"]]},"sourceLabels":["Seoul achalasia consensus guideline (2020), CC BY-NC 4.0","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v4)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v5)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v6)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v7)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v8)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v9)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v10)"],"resultGateAfter":{"id":"gate.achalasia.manometry.3","resultTypeId":"service.esophageal_manometry","pendingLabel":"Esophageal manometry pending","resultNarrative":"Medication reconciliation shows that Sabrina Russo takes oxycodone regularly for back pain and took it before the pressure study. The prescribing team can supervise a medication adjustment, and the patient can maintain oral intake during reassessment.","readiness":"all","allowedServiceRouteIds":["route.esophageal_manometry.outsourced"]},"terminalDispositions":[]},{"id":"node.achalasia.slow-meals.2","questionVariantId":"question.achalasia.manometric-recognition.v3.r5","primaryConceptId":"concept.achalasia.manometric-recognition","currentUpdate":"Medication reconciliation shows that Sabrina Russo takes oxycodone regularly for back pain and took it before the pressure study. The prescribing team can supervise a medication adjustment, and the patient can maintain oral intake during reassessment.","stem":"Which interpretation and next plan best fit Sabrina Russo's record?","showServicePreviews":false,"answerChoices":[{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b03.d1","label":"Achalasia-like spastic pattern; proceed to myotomy planning while continuing the opioid","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Recognizes the tracing but treats it as sufficient proof of a primary irreversible disorder despite a relevant drug exposure.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b03.d3","label":"Distal-spasm pattern; proceed to myotomy planning while continuing the opioid","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Combines an incorrect relaxation-pattern classification with premature invasive treatment planning during the relevant exposure.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"achalasia_3","label":"Achalasia-like spastic pattern; reassess after coordinated opioid withdrawal","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"Recognizes the pattern and the potentially reversible medication-associated contribution before committing to irreversible therapy.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b03.d2","label":"Distal-spasm pattern; coordinate opioid withdrawal and reassessment before myotomy planning","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Notices the medication issue but ignores the abnormal junction relaxation when classifying the tracing.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"The failed/premature body activity with impaired junction relaxation is an achalasia-like spastic pattern. Opioids can produce this phenotype; Chicago v4 advises studying off opioids when possible. Coordinate a clinically appropriate withdrawal/reassessment plan before treating this as established primary disease requiring myotomy. No abrupt cessation or fixed washout is prescribed.","teachingPoint":"The failed/premature body activity with impaired junction relaxation is an achalasia-like spastic pattern. Opioids can produce this phenotype; Chicago v4 advises studying off opioids when possible. Coordinate a clinically appropriate withdrawal/reassessment plan before treating this as established primary disease requiring myotomy. No abrupt cessation or fixed washout is prescribed.","exhibit":{"kind":"keyValue","caption":"Constructed esophageal pressure-study observations; reference ranges are laboratory-specific.","items":[{"label":"Junction measurement","value":"Median relaxation pressure above the laboratory reference range"},{"label":"Most swallows","value":"An early distal contraction begins before the laboratory's latency reference; no normal propagating sequence occurs"},{"label":"Remaining swallows","value":"No distal contraction develops"}]},"sourceLabels":["Seoul achalasia consensus guideline (2020), CC BY-NC 4.0","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v4)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v5)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v6)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v7)"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[{"answerChoiceId":"choice.qq20261102c.b03.d1","kind":"no_terminal_outcome","consequenceNarrative":"The encounter recorded “Achalasia-like spastic pattern; proceed to myotomy planning while continuing the opioid”.","clinicalRationale":"Recognizes the tracing but treats it as sufficient proof of a primary irreversible disorder despite a relevant drug exposure.","sourceLabels":[