eF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Clara Sellers, a 40-year-old woman, visits the clinic with dysphagia to food and drinks and intermittent swallowing-related chest pain. Endoscopy shows no ring or intraluminal mass, and the instrument crosses the junction."},{"id":"profile.achalasia.slow-meals.male.40","prototypeDemographics":{"ageYears":40,"sexLabel":"Male"},"prototypeVitalSigns":{"heartRateBpm":76,"systolicBloodPressureMmHg":118,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"presentation":"Clara Sellers, a 40-year-old man, visits the clinic with dysphagia to food and drinks and intermittent swallowing-related chest pain. Endoscopy shows no ring or intraluminal mass, and the instrument crosses the junction."}],"tutorialEligible":false,"routineEligible":true,"earliestFacilityStage":1,"requiredClinicalSetting":"clinic","requiredCapabilityIds":[],"rewardTierId":"reward.clinic_basic","sourceLabels":["Seoul achalasia consensus guideline (2020), CC BY-NC 4.0"],"decisionNodes":[{"id":"node.achalasia.slow-meals.1","questionVariantId":"question.achalasia.high-resolution-manometry.v3.r8","primaryConceptId":"concept.achalasia.high-resolution-manometry.v3","currentUpdate":"An earlier technically adequate pressure study includes separated swallows and two repeat rapid-swallow sequences. The draft report describes both provocative responses as normal. The recorded observations are shown below.","stem":"Which change should be requested in Clara Sellers's draft report?","showServicePreviews":false,"answerChoices":[{"id":"choice.qq20261102f.b03-context.d2","label":"Revise the reserve conclusion; revise the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"The reserve conclusion needs correction, but the symptom-provoking liquid load does not itself supply evidence for the added outflow revision.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102f.b03-context.d1","label":"Retain the reserve conclusion; revise the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"This accepts suppression as proof of reserve and lets symptom reproduction override the actual junction and pressure observations during loading.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"manometry_3","label":"Revise the reserve conclusion; retain the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"Normal inhibition during the sequence is not evidence of post-sequence augmentation. The repeat responses challenge the reserve conclusion, whereas reproduced discomfort without the supplied outflow findings does not establish liquid-load obstruction.","serviceRequest":{"serviceId":"service.esophageal_manometry"}},{"id":"choice.qq20261102f.b03-context.d3","label":"Retain the reserve conclusion; retain the outflow conclusion","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Baseline propagation and appropriate inhibition do not establish the post-sequence reserve response. Accepting the whole draft misses that distinction.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"A provocative protocol has distinct acquisition phases. Appropriate inhibition while swallowing does not establish augmentation after the sequence; the repeated post-sequence finding warrants correction of the reserve statement. A familiar symptom during rapid drinking does not by itself establish impaired outflow. This report review does not assign a final motor diagnosis or a cause of all symptoms.","teachingPoint":"A provocative protocol has distinct acquisition phases. Appropriate inhibition while swallowing does not establish augmentation after the sequence; the repeated post-sequence finding warrants correction of the reserve statement. A familiar symptom during rapid drinking does not by itself establish impaired outflow. This report review does not assign a final motor diagnosis or a cause of all symptoms.","exhibit":{"kind":"table","caption":"Constructed observations from an earlier study; laboratory comparisons are qualitative.","columnHeaders":["Separated swallows","Rapid-