y not required for that care.","teachingPoint":"Research participation must be voluntary. When a study is optional and not required for ordinary care, declining it should not forfeit that care. Remember to separate the clinical care decision from the research invitation. Making enrollment a condition of routine care pressures the patient instead of supporting an independent choice about participation.","sourceLabels":["Informed Consent in Research","Informed Consent","2023 GEIS Guidelines for gastrointestinal stromal tumors","Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors Treatment (PDQ®), Health Professional Version"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[]},{"id":"node.gs028se.p2e.research-consent.v1.gist.2","questionVariantId":"question.gastric-gist.mutation-guided-neoadjuvant-imatinib.v1.p2e.research-consent.v1","primaryConceptId":"concept.gastric-gist.mutation-guided-neoadjuvant-imatinib","currentUpdate":"The team explains that Julia Walton's ordinary gastric-GIST care does not depend on joining the optional study. The specialist reviews completed core pathology and molecular testing showing GIST with an imatinib-sensitive mutation and no metastatic disease. The large posterior fundus mass abuts the pancreas; the multidisciplinary team expects meaningful shrinkage could avoid combined gastric and pancreatic resection.","stem":"Which multidisciplinary treatment plan best fits Julia Walton's imatinib-sensitive, nonmetastatic gastric GIST when tumor shrinkage could reduce the morbidity of the planned extensive resection?","answerChoices":[{"id":"chemotherapy_1","label":"Give neoadjuvant cytotoxic chemotherapy before resection","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Conventional cytotoxic chemotherapy is not the selected targeted approach for imatinib-sensitive GIST.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"imatinib_1","label":"Plan neoadjuvant imatinib before reassessing resection","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"For an imatinib-sensitive GIST where the multidisciplinary team expects shrinkage to reduce operative morbidity, neoadjuvant imatinib with later resection reassessment is appropriate; size alone would not establish this plan.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"observe_1","label":"Offer symptom-directed care without tumor treatment","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Symptom-only care does not address this large, locally advanced lesion.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"immediate_1","label":"Proceed directly to the planned extensive resection","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Immediate extensive surgery gives up the documented opportunity for morbidity-reducing shrinkage.","serviceRequest":null}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"Plan neoadjuvant imatinib with later reassessment of resection for this mutation-sensitive GIST. The documented opportunity to reduce operative morbidity supports the plan; size alone does not.","teachingPoint":"For an imatinib-sensitive GIST where the multidisciplinary team expects shrinkage to reduce operative morbidity, neoadjuvant imatinib with later resection reassessment is appropriate; size alone would not establish this plan.","sourceLabels":["2023 GEIS Guidelines for gastrointestinal stromal tumors","Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors Treatment (PDQ®), Health Professional Version","Informed Consent in Research","Informed Consent"],"resultGateAfter":null,"terminalDispositions":[{"answerChoiceId":"immediate_1","kind":"no_terminal_outcome","consequenceNarrative":"Review the teaching point and choice rationales.","clinicalRationale":"Plan neoadjuvant imatinib with later reassessment of resection for this mutation-sensitive GIST. The documented opportunity to reduce operative morbidity supports the plan; size alone does not.","sourceLabels":["2023 GEIS Guidelines for gastrointestinal stromal tumors","Gastrointestinal Stromal Tumors Treatment (PDQ®), Health Professional Version","Informed Consent in Research","Informed Consent"]},{"answerChoiceId":"chemotherapy_1","kind":"no_terminal_outcome","consequenceNarrative":"Review th