scopic removal of a polyp in the sigmoid colon. The site now appears as a scar. Malignant cells below the muscularis mucosae form solid groups with very little gland formation."},{"id":"profile.colon-cancer.sigmoid-referral.male.66","prototypeDemographics":{"ageYears":66,"sexLabel":"Male"},"prototypeVitalSigns":{"heartRateBpm":76,"systolicBloodPressureMmHg":118,"diastolicBloodPressureMmHg":74,"temperatureF":98.4,"oxygenSaturationPercent":99},"chiefComplaint":"Polyp pathology review","presentation":"Grace Winter, a 66-year-old man, visits the clinic after endoscopic removal of a polyp in the sigmoid colon. The site now appears as a scar. Malignant cells below the muscularis mucosae form solid groups with very little gland formation."}],"tutorialEligible":false,"routineEligible":true,"earliestFacilityStage":1,"requiredClinicalSetting":"clinic","requiredCapabilityIds":[],"rewardTierId":"reward.clinic_basic","sourceLabels":["ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Colon Cancer","Colon Cancer Treatment (PDQ), Health Professional Version"],"decisionNodes":[{"id":"node.colon-cancer.sigmoid-referral.1","questionVariantId":"question.colon-cancer.preoperative-cross-sectional-staging.v2.r5","primaryConceptId":"concept.colon-cancer.preoperative-cross-sectional-staging.v2","currentUpdate":"The outside contrast CT includes the entire chest, abdomen and pelvis. A solitary 8-mm liver focus is neither uniform water attenuation nor fat and cannot be classified from its single portal-venous acquisition. No other distant lesion is reported. Kidney function is within the laboratory range.","stem":"Which investigation should complete Grace Winter's pretreatment assessment?","answerChoices":[{"id":"ct_cap_2","label":"Liver MRI with contrast","isCorrect":true,"rationale":"Dedicated liver characterization addresses the actual unresolved staging finding without discarding adequate coverage elsewhere.","serviceRequest":{"serviceId":"service.liver_mri"}},{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b14-context.d2","label":"Image-guided liver biopsy","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Tissue can be appropriate in selected indeterminate lesions, but dedicated noninvasive characterization is the preferred next problem-solving step for this small single-phase finding before committing to sampling.","serviceRequest":null},{"id":"choice.qq20261102c.b14-context.d1","label":"Whole-body FDG PET-CT","isCorrect":false,"rationale":"Routine metabolic imaging does not substitute for appropriate anatomic characterization of this indeterminate liver finding.","serviceRequest":{"serviceId":"service.imaging.pet-ct"}}],"shuffleAnswers":true,"explanation":"A complete staging examination may leave a focal lesion unresolved. Dedicated liver MRI is an appropriate next characterization study for a subcentimeter single-phase CT finding in known extrahepatic cancer. PET sensitivity and the value/feasibility of biopsy differ from targeted anatomic characterization; neither is universally forbidden. Retain the adequate prior staging.","teachingPoint":"A complete staging examination may leave a focal lesion unresolved. Dedicated liver MRI is an appropriate next characterization study for a subcentimeter single-phase CT finding in known extrahepatic cancer. PET sensitivity and the value/feasibility of biopsy differ from targeted anatomic characterization; neither is universally forbidden. Retain the adequate prior staging.","sourceLabels":["ASCRS Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Management of Colon Cancer","Colon Cancer Treatment (PDQ), Health Professional Version","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v4)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v5)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v6)","AI-assisted revision: needs_clinician_review (question-authoring.2026-11-02.v7)"],"resultGateAfter":{"id":"gate.colon-cancer.2","resultTypeId":"service.liver_mri","pendingLabel":"Li